
export anchor DraftStoriesWithTheAgentFromTheirRight2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        Right-clicking a story, or any selection of stories, offers "Draft with
        agent". It sends each story to the agent in a draft run of its own, and
        all the runs go in parallel. This builds on the Draft column from the
        story before; it is meant mainly for Draft stories, but works on any
        story in Draft, the Backlog, or Next sprint. The spec has nothing yet on
        drafting several stories at once, on this menu item, or on where a
        story's proposals wait, so all of that is new.

        - A card's menu, which right-click opens at the pointer as
          spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.parts says, has "Draft with
          agent". With several cards selected, right-clicking a selected card
          drafts the whole selection, and the bulk bar has the same button.
        - Each story drafts as "Ask agent" drafts an open story
          (spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.drafting). It runs on its own,
          with no session, and uses the planning prompt in
          spec/scope/harness-integration/baked-prompts/index.pi#BakedSystemPrompts.prompts.
          The run is told the story's title and prompt and asked to turn it into
          a full story: a fuller prompt, the mode that fits, steps, and spec
          citations, grounded as
          spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.specGrounding says.
        - The runs go at the same time, none waiting on another. While a story
          drafts, its card shows the loading state
          (spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.states), "Drafting…" with a
          spinner, plus a Stop item in its menu. A failed run shows its error on
          that card, with "Try again".
        - The side panel holds only one thing at a time
          (spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.layout), so the drafts never
          take it over. When a run ends, its card shows how many proposals are
          waiting, as "3 proposals". Opening the story shows them, as proposal
          cards (spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.proposalCards), beside
          or beneath the story. They are kept until each one is decided, across
          reloads.
        - As spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.proposals says, nothing
          reaches the plan until it is accepted. Accepting rewrites the story
          as proposed, or adds steps to it. A Draft story whose proposal is
          accepted moves to the end of the Backlog.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            Right-clicking a story, or any selection of stories, offers "Draft with
            agent". It sends each story to the agent in a draft run of its own, and
            all the runs go in parallel. This builds on the Draft column from the
            story before; it is meant mainly for Draft stories, but works on any
            story in Draft, the Backlog, or Next sprint. The spec has nothing yet on
            drafting several stories at once, on this menu item, or on where a
            story's proposals wait, so all of that is new.

            - A card's menu, which right-click opens at the pointer as
              spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.parts says, has "Draft with
              agent". With several cards selected, right-clicking a selected card
              drafts the whole selection, and the bulk bar has the same button.
            - Each story drafts as "Ask agent" drafts an open story
              (spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.drafting). It runs on its own,
              with no session, and uses the planning prompt in
              spec/scope/harness-integration/baked-prompts/index.pi#BakedSystemPrompts.prompts.
              The run is told the story's title and prompt and asked to turn it into
              a full story: a fuller prompt, the mode that fits, steps, and spec
              citations, grounded as
              spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.specGrounding says.
            - The runs go at the same time, none waiting on another. While a story
              drafts, its card shows the loading state
              (spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.states), "Drafting…" with a
              spinner, plus a Stop item in its menu. A failed run shows its error on
              that card, with "Try again".
            - The side panel holds only one thing at a time
              (spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.layout), so the drafts never
              take it over. When a run ends, its card shows how many proposals are
              waiting, as "3 proposals". Opening the story shows them, as proposal
              cards (spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.proposalCards), beside
              or beneath the story. They are kept until each one is decided, across
              reloads.
            - As spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.proposals says, nothing
              reaches the plan until it is accepted. Accepting rewrites the story
              as proposed, or adds steps to it. A Draft story whose proposal is
              accepted moves to the end of the Backlog.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            I've updated the spec for "Draft with agent". `piton check` reports no problems, `piton build` succeeded, and the new section appears in the compiled reference.

            **New section: `Planning.draftingWithAgent`** in `spec/scope/planning/index.pi`
            - **Where it appears:** "Draft with agent" is in the menu of every card in Draft, the Backlog, and Next sprint, and it's also a bulk-bar button. Cards in Current sprint or Done, and findings, don't have it.
            - **What each run gets:** each story gets its own sessionless Question run with the planning prompt. The run is told the story's title, prompt, mode and steps.
            - **Parallel runs:** all runs start at once and don't wait on each other. A story that is already drafting is skipped.
            - **On the card while it runs:** "Drafting…" with a spinner, and "Stop" in its menu.
            - **If it fails:** the card shows the error, with "Details" and "Try again".
            - **When it ends:** the card shows "3 proposals" in the accent colour, or "Nothing proposed" muted. Opening the story lists the proposals beneath its fields in the side panel, under "Proposals", with Accept all and Reject all. The drafts never take over the panel.
            - **Persistence:** waiting proposals are saved in `.suspense/planning/proposals/`, one file per story id, so they survive reloads and restarts.
            - **Moving out of Draft:** when any proposal for a Draft story is accepted, the story moves to the end of the Backlog.

            **Edits to existing sections**
            - **`Planning.parts`:** the bulk bar has a "Draft with agent" button. Right-clicking a selected card while more than one is selected turns the menu item into "Draft 3 with agent", which drafts the whole selection. Every other menu item still acts on that card alone.
            - **`Planning.drafting`:** a draft about a story, from "Ask agent" or "Draft with agent", can now propose rewriting it.
            - **`Planning.proposals`:** the block's target can be `new`, a story id, or the new `rewrite`.
            - **`Planning.proposalCards`:** a rewrite card is labelled "Rewrite this story". Accepting it replaces the story's title, prompt, mode and spec citations, but keeps its steps and attachments, and marks the story "Updated by agent". If the story was deleted it falls back to "New story", and a story already sent can't be rewritten.
            - **`Planning.states`:** has a new line for a story drafting with the agent.
            - **`BakedSystemPrompts.prompts`** (in `spec/scope/harness-integration/baked-prompts/index.pi`): `planning.pi` now also covers turning one story into a full story, proposing its rewrite and the steps it needs.

            A few things the prompt left open, which I chose:
            - Steps come back as separate "Add to" proposals rather than inside the rewrite.
            - Drafting a story again while proposals wait adds the new ones after them.
            - A deleted story takes its waiting proposals with it.
            - A duplicate copies none of them.

            The code should follow `Planning.draftingWithAgent`, `Planning.parts`, `Planning.drafting`, `Planning.proposals`, `Planning.proposalCards`, `Planning.states` and `BakedSystemPrompts.prompts`.
            \\\

    sentFrom: DraftStoriesWithTheAgentFromTheirRight

    model: opus
